  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1101 PM CST SUN NOV 22 2026\n\nA cold front is currently moving into northern Mississippi, while\na pre-frontal cluster of showers and a few storms has developed\nacross Central Mississippi, aided by a 700mb vort max. Weakening\nis expected as this activity moves eastward due to very little\ninstability, but PoPs have been increased across the northwestern\nhalf of the area, as the vort max will enable some shower\nactivity to persist (20-50% chances). A low stratus deck\ncontinues to erode westward across our far southeastern counties.\nSince clouds hung around through the day in many areas, fog is\nexpected to continue to develop due to low dew point depressions\nand light winds. While visibilities will improve once the front\nand shower activity move in, the front will only make it through\nour northwest counties prior to sunrise. Therefore, there\ncontinues to be a concern for dense fog development, and will\ncontinue to monitor visibility trends for the potential need for\na dense fog advisory.\n\nThe front will continue to move southeastward through the area\ntomorrow. The continued presence of the 700mb vort max and some\nenhanced 700mb moisture, along with an upper-level jet streak and\nsome weak instability in our far southern counties, will result\nin continued chances for isolated to scattered shower activity\n(20-30% chance). Temperatures will remain near normal in most\nareas as cold air advection lags behind the front. Northerly\nwinds will allow cooler and drier air to advect in Monday night.\nWinds shift to easterly on Tuesday as high pressure slides\neastward from the Midwest to New England, setting up a strong\ncold air damming (CAD) wedge. Temperatures will still manage to\nwarm up on Tuesday ahead of the wedge front.\n\nA fairly complex synoptic pattern sets up Tuesday night through\nThanksgiving. A southern stream shortwave will close off into a\nclosed low at times as it moves eastward along the Gulf Coast,\nwhile a disturbance in the subtropical jet causes a surface low\nto develop between the Yucatan and the Florida Keys. As these two\nsystems interact, strong surface cyclogenesis will take place\nalong the East Coast. An additional northern stream upper low\nwill also drop down. While these systems won't completely phase\ntogether, there is some uncertainty regarding how much partial\nphasing will occur. In terms of Central Alabama weather impacts,\na veering wind profile and associated isentropic lift will result\nin development of stratiform light to moderate rain late Tuesday\nnight into Wednesday morning across Mississippi and far western\nAlabama. There is uncertainty regarding how quickly this rain\nwill make it eastward as some low-level dry air associated with\nthe wedge and a gap between the Gulf Coast and East Coast systems\nlingers across East Alabama. Still expect rain to be in place\nacross much of the area during the peak holiday travel times in\nthe late afternoon and evening hours. The air mass is expected to\nremain too stable for any thunderstorm activity. Most likely\nrainfall amounts will only be an inch or less in most areas, but\nmodels do indicate a locally higher band of beneficial amounts\naround 2 inches setting up either over western Mississippi or\nWest Alabama. Gradient winds will gust up to 30 mph with\npotential for a locally higher gust to 35 mph. Depending on the\nevolution of the upper low, models do indicate some light rain\nmay linger into Thanksgiving morning, but dry and cool conditions\nshould return by around lunch time.\n\nGenerally cool and dry conditions are expected through the end of\nthe week, with west winds causing a warming trend through the\nweekend. An active southwest flow aloft remains in place, but\nconditions remain mainly dry. Some low rain chances may return by\nSunday, but these remain 10% or less at this time.\n\n32/JDavis",
